#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Pomodoro board on a Novation Launchpad Mini MK3.

  row 8 (top)   MODE ROW. One pad per mode, lit bright when selected and dim
                when not. Modes keep their own state and keep running while
                hidden -- a pomodoro started in mode 1 still finishes (and still
                chimes) while you are looking at mode 2.
                RIGHTMOST pad is RED: HOLD 2s to reset the board to default.
                Holding fills the row leftward so you can see it coming, and
                letting go early cancels -- a corner pad is too easy to brush
                for a single press to be allowed to kill a running timer.
  row 7         DAY BAR. How much of today has begun, one pad per three
                hours, deep blue and read-only -- the same bar the daily habit
                tab carries, drawn by the same code.
  rows 6..3     four independent pomodoro timers, one per row.
  row 2         free toggles, press cycles off -> red -> green -> off.
  row 1         break timer. The same state machine as a pomodoro row, but
                8 x 1 min = 8 min, idle light blue instead of white, and it
                fills blue instead of green.

The habit tabs give up their top row to the same kind of bar, each for its own
period: the daily tab shows the day, the weekly one shows the week, a pad per
day. A cell lights as its slice STARTS, so a full bar means you are inside the
last slice -- the weekly bar turns pink for Saturday, while the daily one stays
blue until an hour is left and then counts itself down: yellow blinking slowly
from 23:00, orange at the pomodoro cadence from 23:30, red at twice that from
23:40, rapid red from 23:50, and a panic strobe for the last five minutes.

The machine tab, fourth from the left, is read-only except its bottom row:

  row 7   day bar again
  row 6   RAID members, one pad per physical DISK -- green in sync, amber
          rebuilding or in a degraded array, strobing red failed
  row 5   filesystems: /, /home, Data-1, Fast-1. Green over 100GB free,
          yellow under, red under 30GB, strobing if it is not mounted at all
  row 3   temperatures: CPU, GPU, NVMe, with thresholds per part
  row 1   output to game_stereo | output to the headset (and its mic) | gap |
          EasyEffects on/off | gap | previous | play/pause | next
          Every one of these shows the state the system is actually in, so
          changing the output or the effects anywhere else moves the pads.
  rows 4, 2   reserved, drawn dark

  The output pads show the default sink and nothing else. The EasyEffects pad
  is an on/off switch whose COLOUR says which preset is live: white off, green
  for the room, orange for the headset. Both the preset and the bypass state
  are asked of EasyEffects once a second while this tab is drawn, and not at
  all otherwise -- see read_easyeffects().

Everything on that tab is sampled by a background thread and drawn from a
snapshot. Reading it during a frame would not work: `easyeffects -b 3` alone
takes a quarter of a second and a frame is 50ms. That query is never used for
polling anyway -- it starts a second EasyEffects to talk to the first, which
closes the window the first one has open -- so the bypass state is read out of
the config file EasyEffects writes it to.

The spectrum tab next to it is the audio you are listening to, captured from
the current output's monitor and drawn as eight bands with peak markers. It
follows the output, and the capture only runs while the tab is on screen.

A pomodoro row, left to right, is its own progress bar:

  idle        leftmost pad WHITE, rest dark. Press it to start.
  running     3 minutes per pad, 8 pads = 24 minutes.
              finished pads solid green, the current one flashing.
  elapsed     whole row RED except the last pad, which is GREEN.
                press any RED -> failed, back to idle
                press the GREEN -> claimed, whole row solid green
  claimed     press leftmost -> back to idle, ready to run again

  Habit gestures (there is no separate view mode -- the window is the view):
    click            open the window with that cell selected; clicking the
                     SELECTED cell again closes it, a different one moves the
                     selection
    hold             cycle that habit's state once per second held, so keeping
                     the pad down walks unstarted -> started -> complete -> ...

  Habits cycle through three states:
    0 unstarted  -- the habit's own colour
    1 started    -- RED
    2 complete   -- GREEN

  Holding the leftmost pad of any row for 2s returns that row to idle whatever
  state it is in -- the only way to abandon a pomodoro part-way through. It
  fills the row red as it goes, and letting go early cancels.

  Every tab drives the SAME 64 pads, so a press belongs to the tab it was made
  on and to no other: hold a habit in column 0, switch to the pomodoro tab
  without letting go, and that hold is not an abandon gesture. The selected tab
  is saved with the rest of the state, so a restart puts the pads back to
  meaning what they meant before it.

  What each row means is declared once, in TAB_ROWS: drawing reads it and so
  does press dispatch, so a row cannot come to mean one thing to the eye and
  another to the finger. Rows owned by a read-only widget -- a bar, a meter --
  have no gesture at all, by construction rather than by a guard somebody has
  to remember to write.

The point is that starting costs one press with no decisions, and the row
state is readable from across the room without reading anything.
"""
import mido, time, sys, json, os, threading, subprocess, shutil, signal, enum, re, glob
try:
    import numpy as np                     # spectrum tab only; the rest runs without
except ImportError:
    np = None
from typing import Callable, NamedTuple

# ---- hardware -------------------------------------------------------------
SYSEX_PROGRAMMER = [0x00, 0x20, 0x29, 0x02, 0x0D, 0x0E, 0x01]
SYSEX_LIVE       = [0x00, 0x20, 0x29, 0x02, 0x0D, 0x0E, 0x00]
CH_STATIC, CH_FLASH, CH_PULSE = 0, 1, 2      # MIDI channels select the mode

# The Launchpad's own flash is a fixed 50% duty cycle locked to its internal
# clock, so the in-progress pad is blinked in software instead: mostly lit with
# a short blink off, which reads as "this one is live" rather than "alert".
FLASH_ON, FLASH_OFF = 1.5, 0.3
# One frame. Nothing may blink faster than two frames per phase: the board is
# only redrawn this often, so a shorter phase aliases against the loop and can
# come out looking irregular, or hold still altogether.
TICK = 0.05
# The spectrum runs faster than the rest of the board. Everything else changes
# on a human timescale -- a pomodoro cell every three minutes, a CPU column a
# few times a second -- but audio does not, and 20fps visibly smears it.
# Measured: pw-record delivers fresh samples at 96Hz, analysing them costs
# 0.10ms and drawing them 0.02ms, so the frame rate was the only thing in the
# way.
SPEC_TICK = 0.02

OFF, WHITE, RED, GREEN, BLUE = 0, 3, 5, 21, 45
YELLOW, CYAN = 13, 33
DIM_BLUE, DIM_CYAN = 47, 35              # palette: +2 from a colour is a dim variant

# mode row: one colour per mode; the SELECTED mode shows white instead
MAGENTA, ORANGE, PINK = 53, 9, 57
PURPLE = 49
SKY = 37
TAB_SELECTED = SKY                   # tabs are white; the selected one is sky blue
HAB_FLASH = 0.3                      # in-progress habits alternate every 0.3s

# Tab column -> mode. Columns 3-5 are deliberately empty, and column 7 is the
# reset pad, so modes are addressed by the column they sit on rather than by
# a running index.
M_POMO, M_HAB, M_HAB2, M_MACH, M_SPEC, M_SYS, M_NET = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
TABS = (M_POMO, M_HAB, M_HAB2, M_MACH, M_SPEC, M_SYS, M_NET)
HAB_MODES = (M_HAB, M_HAB2)          # each keeps its own independent set
NET_IFACE = None                     # auto-detected: the default-route interface

# habits occupy everything below the mode row except the top one, which the
# elapsed-time bar has taken over
HAB_ROWS = [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]

# No hold threshold for peeking: the window opens the instant a pad goes down
# and closes on release, so a plain click just flashes it. Waiting to decide
# whether a press was a "hold" cost 350ms of dead time before anything appeared.
HOLD_CYCLE  = 1.0         # hold this long to advance a habit's state
POPUP = os.path.expanduser('~/.local/bin/habit-popup')

# ---- layout ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Programmer mode addresses pads as row*10+col, row 1 = BOTTOM, col 1 = LEFT.
# We think top-down, so logical row 0 is the top physical row (8).
def pad(lrow, lcol):
    return (8 - lrow) * 10 + (lcol + 1)

CELLS      = 8
FUNC_ROW   = 0
RESET_COL  = 7                                # top-right pad
RESET_HOLD = 2.0                              # seconds to hold before it fires

# note -> (row, col). A dict, so a pad that is not on the grid is a miss rather
# than a 64-iteration search that returns None.
RC = {pad(r, c): (r, c) for r in range(8) for c in range(8)}


class Timer(NamedTuple):
    """One timer row. A pomodoro and a break are the same state machine at
    different lengths, so there is one implementation and two of these rather
    than two implementations that can drift apart."""
    step:  int                 # seconds per cell
    idle:  int                 # colour of the leftmost pad when idle
    fill:  int                 # colour it fills with
    chime: str                 # played when the row elapses
    warn:  tuple               # (seconds in, chime) one-shot warning, or None

    @property
    def length(self):
        return self.step * CELLS


# Four strikes for the break and three for a pomodoro, so you can tell which
# one ended without looking. The warning is higher and quicker than either, so
# it cannot be mistaken for a timer actually finishing.
POMODORO = Timer(step=3 * 60, idle=WHITE, fill=GREEN, chime='finish.wav', warn=None)
BREAK    = Timer(step=60, idle=BLUE, fill=BLUE, chime='break.wav',
                 warn=(6 * 60, 'break-warn.wav'))

BAR_ROW    = 1                                # the top row is an elapsed-time bar
POMO_ROWS  = [2, 3, 4, 5]
TOGGLE_ROWS= [6]
BREAK_ROW  = 7
TIMERS     = {**{r: POMODORO for r in POMO_ROWS}, BREAK_ROW: BREAK}
TIMER_ROWS = list(TIMERS)
# system monitor: memory across the bottom row, CPU columns in the rows above
MEM_ROW    = 7
CPU_ROWS   = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]           # drawn bottom-up
METER_ROWS = CPU_ROWS + [MEM_ROW]         # the network meter uses all seven
STATE_FILE = os.path.expanduser('~/.local/share/launchpad-pomodoro.json')
SOUND_DIR  = os.path.expanduser('~/.local/share/launchpad-pomodoro')
PLAYER     = shutil.which('paplay') or shutil.which('pw-play')

# ---- elapsed-time bars ----------------------------------------------------
# The top row of a tab is not part of that tab: it is a bar showing how much of
# a period has begun, one cell per slice. A cell lights as its slice STARTS,
# not when it ends, so a full bar means "you are inside the last slice".
# Lighting on the way out would put the final cell on the stroke of midnight,
# where the reset replaces it with an empty bar in the same instant.
class Stage(NamedTuple):
    """How a FULL bar looks with a given amount of the period left."""
    within: int                        # applies at or below this many seconds left
    colour: int
    on:     float                      # seconds lit
    off:    float                      # seconds dark; 0 means solid


# A full bar holds still in its period's full colour until the last hour, then
# warms and quickens together: a slow yellow blink is a heads-up, orange means
# the half hour is gone, red means minutes. Colour and rate escalate in step,
# so the bar is readable at a glance and from the corner of an eye both, and
# never doubles back -- nothing after this point returns to a cooler colour.
#
# Changing the rate lengthens or shortens the LIT chunk; the dark one stays at
# FLASH_OFF. A gap that grows with the period stops reading as a blink and
# starts reading as a pad that is off. Only the last two stages shorten it,
# where the whole cycle is too short to fit 0.3 s of darkness in.
#
# First match wins, so these are ordered shortest-remaining first.
BAR_STAGES = (
    Stage(5 * 60,  RED,    2 * TICK, 2 * TICK),            # 23:55: panic
    Stage(10 * 60, RED,    0.15, 0.15),                    # 23:50: rapid
    Stage(20 * 60, RED,    FLASH_ON / 2, FLASH_OFF / 2),   # 23:40: twice pomodoro
    Stage(30 * 60, ORANGE, FLASH_ON, FLASH_OFF),           # 23:30: pomodoro cadence
    Stage(60 * 60, YELLOW, FLASH_ON * 2, FLASH_OFF),       # 23:00: half rate, yellow
)


def _into_day(t):
    """Seconds since local midnight."""
    return t.tm_hour * 3600 + t.tm_min * 60 + t.tm_sec


def _into_week(t):
    """Seconds since midnight on Sunday. tm_wday counts from Monday."""
    return ((t.tm_wday + 1) % 7) * 86400 + _into_day(t)


class Period(NamedTuple):
    """A repeating stretch of time, drawn as one row of pads.

    Measured against the local wall clock, so a bar follows the clock on the
    wall through daylight saving rather than counting fixed seconds from some
    past midnight.
    """
    name:    str
    colour:  int                       # while it is filling
    full:    int                       # once every cell is lit
    slices:  int                       # slices in the period, at most CELLS
    step:    int                       # seconds per slice
    into:    Callable                  # seconds into the period, from a struct_time
    flashes: bool                      # escalate through the final hour?

    @property
    def length(self):
        return self.slices * self.step

    @property
    def solid(self):
        """A full bar with time still to run: lit, and holding still."""
        return Stage(None, self.full, 0, 0)

    def bar(self, now=None):
        """(cells lit, seconds left).

        Spare cells -- a week has seven slices but a row has eight pads -- light
        with the last real slice, so the bar reads as full on the last day
        instead of holding a gap that can never close.
        """
        into = self.into(time.localtime(now if now is not None else time.time()))
        lit = int(into // self.step) + 1
        return (CELLS if lit >= self.slices else lit), self.length - into

    def stage(self, left):
        """How a full bar of this period looks with `left` seconds to run.

        A period that does not flash only ever has the one stage: solid, for
        however long it stays full. The weekly bar is full for a whole day, and
        a day of blinking pads is noise rather than urgency.
        """
        if not self.flashes:
            return self.solid
        for st in BAR_STAGES:
            if left <= st.within:
                return st
        return self.solid


# The day stays its own blue once full: the last three hours are not urgent,
# they are just the last three hours. The colour only leaves blue when there is
# an hour left and it starts counting down.
DAY  = Period('day', BLUE, full=BLUE, slices=CELLS, step=3 * 3600,
              into=_into_day, flashes=True)
# The weekly bar does not flash: it is full for a whole day, and a day of
# blinking pads is noise rather than urgency. Pink rather than red for the same
# reason -- a bar that will sit there all Saturday should not be shouting.
WEEK = Period('week', PURPLE, full=PINK, slices=7, step=86400,
              into=_into_week, flashes=False)

# Which bar a tab carries on BAR_ROW. The pomodoro tab and the daily habit tab
# show the same day, drawn by the same code from the same Period.
BARS = {M_POMO: DAY, M_HAB: DAY, M_MACH: DAY, M_HAB2: WEEK}


# ---- machine and audio ----------------------------------------------------
DISK_ROW, FS_ROW, TEMP_ROW, CTRL_ROW = 2, 3, 5, 7
MACH_BLANK = [4, 6]                        # reserved, drawn dark


class Sensor(NamedTuple):
    """A temperature, and where it stops being fine. Thresholds are per part:
    70C is a warm CPU and a cooked NVMe."""
    name:  str
    warm:  int                             # yellow from here
    hot:   int                             # red from here
    panic: int                             # flashing red from here
    hwmon: str                             # /sys/class/hwmon name to look for
    label: str                             # which tempN_label under it


SENSORS = (
    Sensor('cpu',  70, 85,  95, 'k10temp', 'Tctl'),
    Sensor('gpu',  65, 80,  87, '',        ''),        # nvidia-smi, not hwmon
    Sensor('nvme', 55, 70,  80, 'nvme',    'Composite'),
)


class Mount(NamedTuple):
    """A filesystem worth watching, and how empty is too empty."""
    name: str
    path: str


MOUNTS = (Mount('root',   '/'),
          Mount('home',   os.path.expanduser('~')),
          Mount('data',   os.path.expanduser('~/Data-1')),
          Mount('fast',   os.path.expanduser('~/Fast-1')))
FS_LOW, FS_CRIT = 100, 30                  # GB free: yellow under, red under

# Audio devices are matched on a fragment of the PipeWire node name rather than
# the whole thing: the headset's name carries its USB path, which changes when
# it moves port.
GAME_SINK = 'game_stereo'
HEADSET   = 'AT_ATH-M50xSTS'               # sink AND mic; one button picks both
SPOTIFY   = 'org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.spotify'
# EasyEffects writes its bypass state here the moment it changes, which is the
# only way to read it that does not touch the application. Asking the CLI
# (`easyeffects -b 3`) starts a second instance to talk to the first, and that
# closes the window the first one has open -- once a poll, every poll.
EE_CONF = os.path.expanduser('~/.config/easyeffects/db/easyeffectsrc')
# Which preset EasyEffects loads for which output. Read rather than configured
# here: the pad should follow whatever autoload is actually set to, so renaming
# a preset in EasyEffects cannot leave the board lying about it.
EE_AUTOLOAD = os.path.expanduser('~/.local/share/easyeffects/autoload/output')
EE_PRESETS  = os.path.expanduser('~/.local/share/easyeffects/output')
PRESET_HEADSET = ORANGE                    # effects on, wearing the headset
PRESET_MAIN    = GREEN                     # effects on, out through the room


class Button(NamedTuple):
    """One pad on the control row: what it shows and what it does."""
    kind: str                              # sink | effects | media
    arg:  str


# Left to right, one per pad. None is a deliberate gap: the outputs, the
# effects toggle and the transport are three different jobs and a stray press
# should not fire the wrong one.
CONTROLS = (Button('sink', GAME_SINK), Button('sink', HEADSET), None,
            Button('effects', ''), None,
            Button('media', 'Previous'), Button('media', 'PlayPause'),
            Button('media', 'Next'))
SELECTED = BLUE                            # the output you are listening on
PLAYING  = SKY                             # transport, while Spotify is playing
DISK_OK, DISK_SYNC, DISK_FAIL = GREEN, ORANGE, RED


# ---- spectrum -------------------------------------------------------------
SPEC_ROWS = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]          # filled from the bottom
# One hue per column, low to high. The meters are traffic lights -- green up to
# yellow up to red -- so a rainbow reads as a different instrument entirely,
# which is the point: you should know which tab you are on at a glance.
SPEC_HUES = (RED, ORANGE, YELLOW, GREEN, CYAN, SKY, BLUE, MAGENTA)
SPEC_PEAK = WHITE                          # peak hold, falling back slowly
SPEC_LO, SPEC_HI = 40, 12000               # Hz, log spaced across the columns
# Calibrated against Spotify at full volume: bands median about -22dB tilted
# and peak around -8dB, so the row sits half height and tops out on transients
# rather than pinning. Quieter listening drops it a couple of rows, which is
# the point of a meter.
SPEC_FLOOR, SPEC_CEIL = -46.0, -6.0        # dB at the bottom and top of a column
# Music carries most of its energy low down -- measured on this system, the
# bass bands run 1.9dB per band above the treble -- so without a tilt the right
# of the board barely moves. Tilting by slightly less than the measured slope
# evens the row out while leaving bass still visibly the loudest, which is what
# it actually is.
SPEC_TILT = 1.6
SPEC_FALL = 0.6                            # peak hold decay, per second


class Widget(enum.Enum):
    """What a row of pads means on a given tab.

    Every tab drives the same 64 pads, so a row means nothing on its own -- it
    means whatever the tab showing it says it means. Naming those meanings
    keeps rendering and input reading from the same declaration below.
    """
    BAR    = 'bar'                     # read-only elapsed time
    TIMER  = 'timer'
    TOGGLE = 'toggle'
    HABIT  = 'habit'
    CPU    = 'cpu'                     # read-only meters
    MEM    = 'mem'
    NET    = 'net'
    DISK   = 'disk'
    FS     = 'fs'
    TEMP   = 'temp'
    BLANK  = 'blank'                   # claimed, deliberately dark
    CONTROL= 'control'
    SPECTRUM = 'spectrum'


# The layout, stated once. press() dispatches on it and render() draws it, so a
# row cannot come to mean one thing to the eye and another to the finger.
TAB_ROWS = {
    M_POMO: {Widget.TIMER: TIMER_ROWS, Widget.TOGGLE: TOGGLE_ROWS},
    M_HAB:  {Widget.HABIT: HAB_ROWS},
    M_HAB2: {Widget.HABIT: HAB_ROWS},
    M_MACH: {Widget.DISK: [DISK_ROW], Widget.FS: [FS_ROW],
             Widget.TEMP: [TEMP_ROW], Widget.CONTROL: [CTRL_ROW],
             Widget.BLANK: MACH_BLANK},
    M_SPEC: {Widget.SPECTRUM: SPEC_ROWS},
    M_SYS:  {Widget.CPU: CPU_ROWS, Widget.MEM: [MEM_ROW]},
    M_NET:  {Widget.NET: METER_ROWS},
}
for _m in BARS:                        # a tab with a bar gives up that row
    TAB_ROWS[_m][Widget.BAR] = [BAR_ROW]


def widget_at(mode, row):
    """What `row` means on `mode`, or None if that tab leaves it dark."""
    for widget, rows in TAB_ROWS.get(mode, {}).items():
        if row in rows:
            return widget
    return None


def _check_layout():
    """Two widgets on one tab must never claim the same row.

    Sharing a row is how a press comes to mean two things at once, which is the
    bug this board has had more than any other -- worth a startup failure
    rather than a silent misfire hours later.
    """
    for mode, widgets in TAB_ROWS.items():
        claimed = {}
        for widget, rows in widgets.items():
            for r in rows:
                if r in claimed:
                    raise RuntimeError(f'tab {mode}: row {r} is both '
                                       f'{claimed[r].value} and {widget.value}')
                claimed[r] = widget


_check_layout()


def sh(*cmd, timeout=4):
    """Run a command and return its stdout, or '' if anything at all goes
    wrong. Only ever called from the poller thread."""
    try:
        return subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=timeout).stdout
    except Exception:
        return ''


def spawn(*cmd):
    """Fire and forget. A press must never wait on pactl or dbus: the thread
    that reads the pads is the thread that draws them."""
    try:
        subprocess.Popen(cmd, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f'{cmd[0]}: {e!r}', flush=True)


def match(fragment, names):
    """The first device whose node name contains `fragment`, or ''.

    Devices are matched on a fragment because a USB name carries the port it
    is plugged into, and moving the headset to another socket renames it.
    """
    return next((n for n in names if fragment in n), '')


DISK_RANK = {'ok': 0, 'sync': 1, 'fail': 2}


def parse_mdstat(text):
    """((disk, state), ...) from /proc/mdstat, worst state per disk.

    Keyed by the whole disk rather than the partition: three arrays can live
    on the same pair of drives, and it is the drive that dies. A disk that has
    vanished entirely cannot be listed -- mdstat only names what is still
    there -- but its array reads as degraded, so the surviving half goes amber
    and the row still says something is wrong.
    """
    out = {}
    for block in re.split(r'\n(?=\S)', text):
        head = block.split('\n')[0]
        if not re.match(r'md\w+\s*:', head):
            continue
        status = re.search(r'\[([U_]+)\]', block)
        degraded = bool(status and '_' in status.group(1))
        busy = bool(re.search(r'\b(resync|recovery|reshape|check)\b', block))
        for member, flags in re.findall(r'(\w+?)\[\d+\](\([A-Z]\))?', head):
            disk = re.sub(r'p?\d+$', '', member)
            if flags in ('(F)', '(W)'):
                state = 'fail'
            elif degraded or busy or flags == '(S)':
                state = 'sync'
            else:
                state = 'ok'
            if DISK_RANK[state] >= DISK_RANK.get(out.get(disk, 'ok'), 0):
                out[disk] = state
    return tuple(sorted(out.items()))


def free_gb(path):
    """Gigabytes free on the filesystem holding `path`, or None if it is not
    there at all -- which for these four means something is unmounted."""
    try:
        st = os.statvfs(path)
        return st.f_bavail * st.f_frsize / 1e9
    except Exception:
        return None


def hwmon_temp(name, label):
    """Degrees from the hwmon device called `name`, channel `label`.

    Found by name, not by number: hwmon0 is the NVMe today and could be the
    wifi card after the next reboot.
    """
    for d in sorted(glob.glob('/sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*')):
        try:
            if open(os.path.join(d, 'name')).read().strip() != name:
                continue
            for lf in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(d, 'temp*_label'))):
                if open(lf).read().strip() == label:
                    return int(open(lf[:-6] + '_input').read()) / 1000.0
        except Exception:
            continue
    return None


def headset_preset():
    """The preset EasyEffects autoloads for the headset, or ''.

    Taken from the autoload files EasyEffects writes itself, so the pad follows
    whatever is actually bound rather than a preset name copied in here that
    could quietly stop matching.
    """
    try:
        for name in os.listdir(EE_AUTOLOAD):
            if not name.endswith('.json'):
                continue
            with open(os.path.join(EE_AUTOLOAD, name)) as fh:
                entry = json.load(fh)
            if HEADSET in entry.get('device', ''):
                return entry.get('preset-name', '')
    except Exception:
        pass
    return ''


def gpu_temp():
    out = sh('nvidia-smi', '--query-gpu=temperature.gpu', '--format=csv,noheader')
    try:
        return float(out.strip().splitlines()[0])
    except Exception:
        return None


class Snapshot(NamedTuple):
    """Everything the machine tab draws, as of the last time it was read."""
    disks:   tuple = ()                # ((sda, ok|sync|fail), ...)
    mounts:  tuple = ()                # ((Mount, GB free or None), ...)
    temps:   tuple = ()                # ((Sensor, degrees or None), ...)
    sinks:   tuple = ()                # node names currently available
    sources: tuple = ()
    sink:    str = ''                  # the default sink, however it got set
    running: bool = False              # EasyEffects is up at all
    effects: bool = False              # ...and not bypassed
    preset:  str = ''                  # the output preset it has loaded
    headset_preset: str = ''           # ...and the one autoload gives the headset
    playing: bool = False              # Spotify


class Machine:
    """The machine tab's data, sampled in the background.

    Every reading here costs a syscall at best and a subprocess at worst --
    `easyeffects -b 3` alone is a quarter of a second -- and the board is
    redrawn twenty times a second. So nothing is read during a frame: this
    thread refreshes a snapshot on its own cadence and render() draws whatever
    it last saw. A reading a few seconds stale is fine; a stalled board is not.
    """
    AUDIO, EFFECTS, TEMPS, DISKS = 1.0, 5.0, 5.0, 30.0
    PRESET = 1.0                       # how often, while the tab is on screen
    PRESET_IDLE = 3.0                  # stop asking this long after the last frame
    SETTLE = 0.35                      # let a press land before reading it back

    def __init__(self):
        self.snap = Snapshot()
        self.watched = 0.0             # when the tab was last drawn
        self.recheck_at = 0.0          # a press is waiting to be read back
        self._due = {}                 # job -> when it last ran

    def start(self):
        threading.Thread(target=self._loop, daemon=True).start()

    def jobs(self):
        return (('easyeffects', self.PRESET, self.read_easyeffects),
                ('audio', self.AUDIO, self.read_audio),
                ('effects', self.EFFECTS, self.read_effects),
                ('temps', self.TEMPS, self.read_temps),
                ('disks', self.DISKS, self.read_disks))

    def _run(self, key, read, now):
        self._due[key] = now
        try:
            read()
        except Exception as e:
            print(f'machine {key}: {e!r}', flush=True)

    def poll_once(self, now=None):
        """One pass of the poller. Split out so it can be tested."""
        now = now if now is not None else time.time()
        if self.recheck_at and now >= self.recheck_at:
            # a press is waiting to be read back: the two readers that can see
            # what it did, ahead of their own cadence
            self.recheck_at = 0.0
            self._run('audio', self.read_audio, now)
            self._run('easyeffects', self.read_easyeffects, now)
        for key, every, read in self.jobs():
            if now - self._due.get(key, 0.0) >= every:
                self._run(key, read, now)

    def _loop(self):
        while True:
            self.poll_once()
            time.sleep(0.25)

    def recheck(self, delay=SETTLE):
        """A press has been fired off; read the result back shortly.

        Nothing on the control row assumes its own press worked. The pads show
        what pactl and EasyEffects say, always, so a command that fails or is
        overridden a moment later cannot leave the board asserting something
        that is not true. The delay is for the command to land: it is fired and
        forgotten, and pactl and EasyEffects both take a moment to act on it.
        """
        self.recheck_at = time.time() + delay

    def assume(self, **kw):
        """Record what was just read. Never used to record what was just asked
        for -- see recheck()."""
        self.snap = self.snap._replace(**kw)

    # ---- readers ---------------------------------------------------------
    def read_audio(self):
        def names(kind):
            return tuple(l.split('\t')[1] for l in sh('pactl', 'list', 'short', kind).splitlines()
                         if '\t' in l and not l.split('\t')[1].endswith('.monitor'))
        self.assume(sinks=names('sinks'), sources=names('sources'),
                    sink=sh('pactl', 'get-default-sink').strip(),
                    playing='Playing' in sh(
                        'gdbus', 'call', '--session', '--dest', SPOTIFY,
                        '--object-path', '/org/mpris/MediaPlayer2', '--method',
                        'org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties.Get',
                        'org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.Player', 'PlaybackStatus'))

    def read_effects(self):
        """Running, and not bypassed.

        Both come from outside EasyEffects: `pgrep`, and the config file it
        writes its bypass state into. Asking the application itself
        (`easyeffects -b 3`) starts a second instance to talk to the first,
        which closes the window the first one has open -- fine as the price of
        a deliberate press, unacceptable once every poll.
        """
        running = bool(sh('pgrep', '-x', 'easyeffects').strip())
        state = dict(running=running, headset_preset=headset_preset())
        if not running:
            state['effects'] = False       # nothing running, nothing processing
        self.assume(**state)

    def attend(self, now=None):
        """The machine tab is on screen. Only then is the preset worth the
        process it costs to ask for."""
        self.watched = now if now is not None else time.time()

    def read_easyeffects(self):
        """Preset AND bypass, asked of EasyEffects itself, in one process.

        Both come from here because both lie everywhere else: the config file
        records them but writes lazily -- measured still naming the previous
        preset a minute after autoload had switched it -- and reading bypass
        from that file is what made a pressed pad spring back to green, the
        stale file overwriting the press it had just been told about.

        Every second while the tab is on screen, and nothing else matters --
        not even that the query closes the EasyEffects window if one is open.
        A pad that quietly disagrees with the UI is worse than a window that
        shuts: the shut window is at least obvious, and leaving the tab stops
        it. Nobody looking means nobody asking, so this costs nothing on any
        other tab.
        """
        if time.time() - self.watched > self.PRESET_IDLE:
            return                                  # nobody is looking
        if not self.snap.running:
            return                                  # asking would start it
        # -b 3 reports bypass, -a output the preset, and one invocation can do
        # both: two would be two Qt startups, and racing them returned empty
        lines = [l.strip() for l in
                 sh('easyeffects', '-b', '3', '-a', 'output').splitlines() if l.strip()]
        if not lines:
            return
        state = {}
        if lines[-1] in ('1', '2'):                 # 1 bypassed, 2 running
            state['effects'] = lines[-1] == '2'
            lines = lines[:-1]
        if lines:
            state['preset'] = lines[0]
        self.assume(**state)

    def read_temps(self):
        readings = []
        for s in SENSORS:
            readings.append((s, gpu_temp() if s.name == 'gpu'
                             else hwmon_temp(s.hwmon, s.label)))
        self.assume(temps=tuple(readings))

    def read_disks(self):
        try:
            mdstat = open('/proc/mdstat').read()
        except Exception:
            mdstat = ''
        self.assume(disks=parse_mdstat(mdstat),
                    mounts=tuple((m, free_gb(m.path)) for m in MOUNTS))

    # ---- actions ---------------------------------------------------------
    def set_effects(self, on):
        """Turn processing on however it was off: a bypassed EasyEffects is
        unbypassed, a stopped one is started. Turning it off is bypass, not
        quitting -- the sink stays and the streams do not have to move."""
        if on and not self.snap.running:
            spawn('easyeffects', '--gapplication-service')
        elif self.snap.running:
            spawn('easyeffects', '-b', '1' if not on else '2')
        self.recheck()                 # read back what it did, never assume

    def toggle_effects(self):
        self.set_effects(not self.snap.effects)


class Spectrum:
    """The spectrum of whatever you are actually listening to.

    Captured from the default sink's monitor, so it hears the output after
    EasyEffects rather than before, and follows the output when it changes.

    Capture runs only while the tab is on screen. bands() starts it, and
    reap() -- called every frame from tick(), whichever tab is up -- stops it
    once nothing has asked for a couple of seconds. Sitting on the pomodoro
    tab should not cost a recording stream.
    """
    RATE, WINDOW, IDLE = 24000, 1024, 2.0

    def __init__(self):
        self.proc = None
        self.target = ''
        self.raw = b''
        self.peaks = [0.0] * CELLS
        self.asked = 0.0
        self.seen = 0.0                    # when the peaks last fell back
        self._lock = threading.Lock()
        self._edges = None

    # ---- capture ---------------------------------------------------------
    def _start(self, target):
        self._stop()
        self.target = target
        try:
            self.proc = subprocess.Popen(
                # stream.capture.sink is what makes this the sink's monitor
                # rather than a source: without it pw-record connects happily,
                # streams at the right rate, and delivers digital silence.
                ['pw-record', '--target', target, '-P', 'stream.capture.sink=true',
                 f'--rate={self.RATE}', '--channels=1', '--format=f32',
                 '--latency=10ms', '-'],
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        except Exception as e:
            print(f'pw-record: {e!r}', flush=True)
            self.proc = None
            return
        threading.Thread(target=self._reader, args=(self.proc,), daemon=True).start()

    def _stop(self):
        proc, self.proc = self.proc, None
        self.raw = b''
        if proc:
            try:
                proc.kill()
            except Exception:
                pass

    def _reader(self, proc):
        """Keep the newest window of samples and nothing else. Falling behind
        would show a spectrum of what was playing a minute ago.

        read1() rather than read(): read() waits for the full count and so
        held the buffer a whole extra chunk behind, 71Hz instead of the 96Hz
        pw-record was actually delivering.
        """
        need = self.WINDOW * 4
        buf = b''
        try:
            while proc.poll() is None:
                chunk = proc.stdout.read1(8192)
                if not chunk:
                    break
                buf = (buf + chunk)[-need:]
                with self._lock:
                    self.raw = buf
        except Exception:
            pass

    def reap(self, now=None):
        """Stop capturing if the tab is no longer being drawn."""
        now = now if now is not None else time.time()
        if self.proc and now - self.asked > self.IDLE:
            self._stop()

    # ---- analysis --------------------------------------------------------
    def edges(self):
        """FFT bin index at each band boundary: log spaced, because octaves
        are what ears hear and a linear split would put six of eight columns
        above 6kHz, where music mostly is not."""
        if self._edges is None:
            step = (SPEC_HI / SPEC_LO) ** (1 / CELLS)
            hz = [SPEC_LO * step ** i for i in range(CELLS + 1)]
            self._edges = [max(1, int(f * self.WINDOW / self.RATE)) for f in hz]
        return self._edges

    def bands(self, target, now=None):
        """(levels, peaks), each CELLS values from 0 to 1."""
        now = now if now is not None else time.time()
        self.asked = now
        if not target:
            return [0.0] * CELLS, [0.0] * CELLS
        if not self.proc or target != self.target:
            self._start(target)
        with self._lock:
            raw = self.raw
        levels = [0.0] * CELLS
        if np is not None and len(raw) >= self.WINDOW * 4:
            x = np.frombuffer(raw[-self.WINDOW * 4:], dtype=np.float32)
            win = np.hanning(len(x))
            # scaled so a full-scale sine reads 0 dB whatever the window
            # length: without it every band pins to the top of the column
            mag = np.abs(np.fft.rfft(x * win)) * 2 / win.sum()
            edge = self.edges()
            for c in range(CELLS):
                lo, hi = edge[c], max(edge[c] + 1, edge[c + 1])
                # energy across the band, not the average of its bins: an
                # average divides a tone by however many bins the band happens
                # to span, which makes the wide top bands read quiet
                band = mag[lo:min(hi, len(mag))]
                db = 20 * np.log10(float(np.sqrt(np.sum(band ** 2))) + 1e-9)
                db += SPEC_TILT * c
                levels[c] = min(1.0, max(0.0, (db - SPEC_FLOOR) / (SPEC_CEIL - SPEC_FLOOR)))
        fall = SPEC_FALL * max(0.0, now - self.seen) if self.seen else 0.0
        self.seen = now
        self.peaks = [max(l, p - fall) for l, p in zip(levels, self.peaks)]
        return levels, list(self.peaks)


def ding(name):
    """Fire and forget -- never wait on audio from the input loop."""
    f = os.path.join(SOUND_DIR, name)
    if PLAYER and os.path.exists(f):
        try:
            subprocess.Popen([PLAYER, f],
                             stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        except Exception:
            pass

IDLE, RUNNING, ELAPSED, CLAIMED = 'idle', 'running', 'elapsed', 'claimed'


class Board:
    def __init__(self, out):
        self.out = out
        self.shown = {}                        # pad -> (channel, colour)
        self.rows = {r: {'state': IDLE, 'started': 0.0} for r in TIMER_ROWS}
        self.toggles = {}                      # (row,col) -> 0/1/2
        # note -> (time it went down, mode it was pressed in). The MODE MATTERS:
        # every tab reuses the same 64 physical pads, so note 61 is both habit
        # cell (2,0) and pomodoro row 2's start pad. A press must only ever be
        # read as a gesture by the tab it was made on.
        self.held = {}
        self.mode = M_POMO
        # one habit table per habit mode: daily and weekly are unrelated boards
        self.habit_sets = {str(m): {} for m in HAB_MODES}
        self._hold_count = {}                  # note -> cycles already fired while held
        self._popup = None                     # resident peek window (daemon)
        self._popup_mode = M_HAB               # which table the window is showing
        self._editing = None                   # (r,c) while the edit window is up
        self._cpu_prev = None
        self.machine = Machine()
        self.spectrum = Spectrum()
        self._dirty = False
        self._lock = threading.Lock()
        self.load()
        # Saving must never block input. The state file lives on /home, which is
        # an md array: while it is being scrubbed a single write was measured at
        # 2.4 SECONDS, which showed up as pads lighting half a second after the
        # press. A background writer decouples the two.
        threading.Thread(target=self._writer, daemon=True).start()
        self.machine.start()
        self._start_popup()

    def _start_popup(self):
        """Keep one popup process resident. Spawning a fresh python+tk per peek
        cost ~200ms, which read as lag."""
        try:
            env = dict(os.environ)
            env.setdefault('DISPLAY', ':0')
            self._popup = subprocess.Popen(
                [POPUP], stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                text=True, env=env, bufsize=1)
            threading.Thread(target=self._popup_reader, daemon=True).start()
            self._popup_send_table()
        except Exception:
            self._popup = None

    def _popup_send_table(self, cmd='data'):
        """Push the table the window is meant to be showing.

        Addressed by _popup_mode, not by self.mode: self.habits is {} whenever
        a non-habit tab is selected, so sending self.habits from here blanked
        the window every time the board happened to be on the pomodoro tab.
        """
        self._popup_cmd(f'{cmd}\t' + json.dumps(self.habit_table(self._popup_mode)))

    def _popup_reader(self):
        """Edits made in the window come back as: set<TAB>r<TAB>c<TAB>name<TAB>colour

        Runs on its own thread. It writes into the table the window was given
        (_popup_mode), never into self.habits, which follows the selected tab
        and would send an edit to the wrong board -- or, on a non-habit tab, to
        a throwaway dict where it was silently lost.
        """
        pr = self._popup
        try:
            for line in pr.stdout:
                f = line.rstrip('\n').split('\t')
                mode = self._popup_mode
                if f[0] == 'closed':
                    self._editing = None
                    continue
                if len(f) == 4 and f[0] == 'state':
                    r, c, st = int(f[1]), int(f[2]), int(f[3])
                    h = dict(self.habit(r, c, mode))
                    if h.get('name'):
                        h['state'] = st
                        self.set_habit(r, c, h, mode)
                    continue
                if len(f) == 5 and f[0] == 'set':
                    r, c, name, colour = int(f[1]), int(f[2]), f[3].strip(), int(f[4])
                    old = self.habit(r, c, mode)
                    self.set_habit(r, c, {'name': name, 'colour': colour,
                                          'state': old.get('state', 0) if name else 0}, mode)
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"popup reader stopped: {e!r}", flush=True)

    def _popup_cmd(self, line):
        if not self._popup or self._popup.poll() is not None:
            self._start_popup()                # it died; bring it back
        try:
            self._popup.stdin.write(line + '\n')
            self._popup.stdin.flush()
        except Exception:
            self._popup = None

    # --- persistence: a timer should survive restarting this program --------
    def load(self):
        try:
            with open(STATE_FILE) as fh:
                d = json.load(fh)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return                             # first run; defaults are correct
        except Exception as e:
            # Never silently start from defaults on top of a file we could not
            # read: the background writer would then overwrite a recoverable
            # state file with an empty board a second later.
            print(f"state file unreadable ({e!r}); kept as {STATE_FILE}.bad",
                  flush=True)
            try:
                os.replace(STATE_FILE, STATE_FILE + '.bad')
            except Exception:
                pass
            return
        try:
            for k, v in d.get('rows', {}).items():
                if int(k) in self.rows: self.rows[int(k)] = v
            self.toggles = {tuple(map(int, k.split(','))): v
                            for k, v in d.get('toggles', {}).items()}
            # The selected tab decides what every pad below the top row DOES.
            # Coming back up on the pomodoro tab after a restart meant the next
            # press aimed at a habit landed on a pomodoro row instead, and on an
            # elapsed row that press means "failed" -- it wiped the timer.
            if d.get('mode') in TABS:
                self.mode = self._popup_mode = d['mode']
            if self.mode not in HAB_MODES:
                self._popup_mode = M_HAB
            self.habit_sets = d.get('habit_sets') or {str(m): {} for m in HAB_MODES}
            if d.get('schema', 1) < 2:
                # tabs were reordered; daily/weekly used to live at 2 and 3
                old = dict(self.habit_sets)
                self.habit_sets = {str(M_HAB): old.get('2', {}),
                                   str(M_HAB2): old.get('3', {})}
            if 'habits' in d and not self.habit_sets.get(str(M_HAB)):
                self.habit_sets[str(M_HAB)] = d['habits']      # migrate older layout
            for m in HAB_MODES:
                self.habit_sets.setdefault(str(m), {})
            if d.get('schema', 1) < 3:
                for tbl in self.habit_sets.values():
                    for h in tbl.values():
                        if 'state' not in h:
                            h['state'] = 2 if h.pop('done', False) else 0
                self._dirty = True             # write the migrated layout out now
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"state file only partly loaded: {e!r}", flush=True)

    def save(self):
        """Mark dirty; the background thread does the actual write."""
        self._dirty = True

    def _writer(self):
        while True:
            time.sleep(1.0)
            self._flush_if_dirty()

    def _flush_if_dirty(self):
        """One pass of the writer loop. Split out so it can be tested."""
        if not self._dirty:
            return False
        # Clear the flag only once the bytes are on disk. Clearing it first
        # meant any failed write -- a dumps() racing the popup reader thread, a
        # full disk -- threw the change away with nothing left to retry from.
        if self._flush():
            self._dirty = False
            return True
        return False

    def _flush(self):
        try:
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(STATE_FILE), exist_ok=True)
            with self._lock:
                blob = json.dumps({'rows': {str(k): v for k, v in self.rows.items()},
                                   'toggles': {f'{r},{c}': v for (r, c), v in self.toggles.items()},
                                   'habit_sets': self.habit_sets,
                                   'mode': self.mode,
                                   'schema': 3})
            tmp = STATE_FILE + '.tmp'
            with open(tmp, 'w') as f:
                f.write(blob)
            os.replace(tmp, STATE_FILE)
            return True
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"could not write state: {e!r}", flush=True)
            return False

    # --- drawing -----------------------------------------------------------
    def set(self, p, colour, channel=CH_STATIC):
        if self.shown.get(p) == (channel, colour):
            return                              # don't spam unchanged pads
        self.shown[p] = (channel, colour)
        self.out.send(mido.Message('note_on', channel=channel, note=p, velocity=colour))

    def flash(self, p, colour, on=FLASH_ON, off=FLASH_OFF):
        period = on + off
        lit = (time.time() % period) < on
        self.set(p, colour if lit else OFF, CH_STATIC)

    def clear(self):
        for lr in range(8):
            for lc in range(8):
                self.set(pad(lr, lc), OFF)

    def held_since(self, note, mode=None):
        """When this pad went down -- but only if it was pressed on the tab we
        are asking about, otherwise None.

        Every tab reuses the same 64 pads, so note 61 is habit cell (2,0) AND
        pomodoro row 2's start pad. Reading self.held without checking which tab
        the press was made on meant holding a habit in column 0 and then tapping
        the pomodoro tab silently abandoned that pomodoro row -- the hold-to-
        abandon timer in render() saw a two-second-old entry the moment the
        pomodoro tab drew its first frame.
        """
        got = self.held.get(note)
        if not got:
            return None
        t0, pressed_in = got
        return t0 if pressed_in == (self.mode if mode is None else mode) else None

    def render(self):
        """One frame. Draws only -- tick() owns every state transition."""
        self.render_tabs()
        bar = BARS.get(self.mode)
        if bar:
            self.render_bar(bar)
        if self.mode == M_MACH:
            return self.render_machine()
        if self.mode == M_SPEC:
            return self.render_spectrum()
        if self.mode == M_SYS:
            return self.render_sys()
        if self.mode == M_NET:
            return self.render_net()
        if self.mode in HAB_MODES:
            return self.render_habits()
        self.render_timers()
        self.render_toggles()

    def render_tabs(self):
        """Mode row: one pad per mode (sky = selected), red reset on the right.

        While the reset pad is held the row fills leftward as a 2 s progress
        bar. That fill is feedback only -- reset fires on release, in
        release(), so one pad can serve both a tap and a hold.
        """
        held_for = 0.0
        t0 = self.held_since(pad(FUNC_ROW, RESET_COL))
        if t0: held_for = time.time() - t0
        lit = int((held_for / RESET_HOLD) * RESET_COL) if held_for else 0
        for c in range(CELLS):
            if c == RESET_COL:                     col = RED
            elif lit and c >= RESET_COL - lit:     col = RED
            elif c in TABS and not lit:            col = TAB_SELECTED if c == self.mode else WHITE
            else:                                  col = OFF
            self.set(pad(FUNC_ROW, c), col)

    def render_bar(self, period):
        """BAR_ROW: how much of `period` has begun. See Period."""
        lit, left = period.bar()
        stage = period.stage(left) if lit >= CELLS else None
        colour = stage.colour if stage else period.colour
        for c in range(CELLS):
            p = pad(BAR_ROW, c)
            if c >= lit:                self.set(p, OFF)
            elif stage and stage.off:   self.flash(p, colour, stage.on, stage.off)
            else:                       self.set(p, colour)

    def render_spectrum(self):
        """Eight bands, filled from the bottom, one hue per band, with a peak
        that hangs above the bar and falls back."""
        levels, peaks = self.spectrum.bands(self.machine.snap.sink)
        rows = list(reversed(SPEC_ROWS))                  # index 0 is the bottom
        for c in range(CELLS):
            lit = int(round(levels[c] * len(rows)))
            peak = int(round(peaks[c] * len(rows)))
            for i, r in enumerate(rows):
                if i < lit:                colour = SPEC_HUES[c]
                elif peak and i == peak - 1: colour = SPEC_PEAK
                else:                      colour = OFF
                self.set(pad(r, c), colour)

    def render_machine(self):
        """Disks, filesystems, temperatures and the audio controls."""
        self.machine.attend()
        snap = self.machine.snap
        self.render_disks(snap)
        self.render_filesystems(snap)
        self.render_temps(snap)
        self.render_controls(snap)
        for r in MACH_BLANK:                   # reserved, and drawn dark so a
            for c in range(CELLS):             # tab switch cannot leave the
                self.set(pad(r, c), OFF)       # previous tab's colours behind

    def alarm(self, p, colour=RED):
        """The strobe kept for things that are actually wrong."""
        self.flash(p, colour, 2 * TICK, 2 * TICK)

    def render_disks(self, snap):
        """One pad per physical disk: green in sync, amber rebuilding or in a
        degraded array, strobing red failed."""
        for c in range(CELLS):
            p = pad(DISK_ROW, c)
            if c >= len(snap.disks):
                self.set(p, OFF)
            elif snap.disks[c][1] == 'fail':
                self.alarm(p, DISK_FAIL)
            else:
                self.set(p, DISK_SYNC if snap.disks[c][1] == 'sync' else DISK_OK)

    def render_filesystems(self, snap):
        """One pad per filesystem, by how much room is left on it. A mount that
        cannot be read at all strobes: these four are always mounted, so a
        missing one is news."""
        for c in range(CELLS):
            p = pad(FS_ROW, c)
            if c >= len(snap.mounts):
                self.set(p, OFF)
                continue
            free = snap.mounts[c][1]
            if free is None:        self.alarm(p)
            elif free < FS_CRIT:    self.set(p, RED)
            elif free < FS_LOW:     self.set(p, YELLOW)
            else:                   self.set(p, GREEN)

    def render_temps(self, snap):
        """CPU, GPU, NVMe. Thresholds are per part -- 70C is a warm CPU and a
        cooked NVMe -- so the colours mean the same thing across the row."""
        for c in range(CELLS):
            p = pad(TEMP_ROW, c)
            if c >= len(snap.temps):
                self.set(p, OFF)
                continue
            sensor, t = snap.temps[c]
            if t is None:            self.set(p, OFF)      # nothing to read
            elif t >= sensor.panic:  self.alarm(p)
            elif t >= sensor.hot:    self.set(p, RED)
            elif t >= sensor.warm:   self.set(p, YELLOW)
            else:                    self.set(p, GREEN)

    def render_controls(self, snap):
        """Output, effects and transport. Every one of these shows the state
        the system is actually in, not the last thing this board asked for:
        change the output in the tray applet and the pads follow.
        """
        for c in range(CELLS):
            p = pad(CTRL_ROW, c)
            b = CONTROLS[c] if c < len(CONTROLS) else None
            if b is None:
                self.set(p, OFF)                           # deliberate gap
            elif b.kind == 'sink':
                name = match(b.arg, snap.sinks)
                if not name:   self.set(p, OFF)            # unplugged, or gone
                else:          self.set(p, SELECTED if name == snap.sink else WHITE)
            elif b.kind == 'effects':
                # Off is off whichever preset would have been loaded; on says
                # which one is, because getting the room's EQ in headphones
                # sounds wrong in a way that is easy to miss and hard to place.
                if not snap.effects:
                    self.set(p, WHITE)
                elif snap.preset and snap.preset == snap.headset_preset:
                    self.set(p, PRESET_HEADSET)
                else:
                    self.set(p, PRESET_MAIN)
            else:
                self.set(p, PLAYING if b.arg == 'PlayPause' and snap.playing else WHITE)

    def control_press(self, col):
        """Act on the control row.

        Every action is fired and forgotten, and then read back: a press
        schedules a re-read rather than recording what it asked for, so the
        pads show what pactl and EasyEffects say and nothing else. Assuming a
        press worked is how the pad came to sit on green while EasyEffects was
        bypassed -- the board asserting a state instead of reporting one.
        """
        b = CONTROLS[col] if col < len(CONTROLS) else None
        snap = self.machine.snap
        if b is None:
            return
        if b.kind == 'sink':
            name = match(b.arg, snap.sinks)
            if not name:
                return                                     # nothing to switch to
            spawn('pactl', 'set-default-sink', name)
            if b.arg == HEADSET:
                # the headset is a sink and a microphone, and picking it means
                # picking both -- otherwise you switch to it and still record
                # off whatever was selected before
                mic = match(HEADSET, snap.sources)
                if mic:
                    spawn('pactl', 'set-default-source', mic)
            # Nothing is done to EasyEffects here: it autoloads a preset per
            # output device, so switching the sink switches the processing by
            # itself. Bypassing for the headset -- which is what this did
            # before there was a headset preset -- would make that preset
            # unreachable from the board.
            self.machine.recheck()
        elif b.kind == 'effects':
            self.machine.toggle_effects()                  # recheck()s itself
        else:
            spawn('gdbus', 'call', '--session', '--dest', SPOTIFY,
                  '--object-path', '/org/mpris/MediaPlayer2',
                  '--method', f'org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.Player.{b.arg}')
            if b.arg == 'PlayPause':
                self.machine.recheck()

    def render_timers(self):
        """Every timer row on the pomodoro tab, each its own progress bar."""
        now = time.time()
        for r in TIMER_ROWS:
            st = self.rows[r]
            s = st['state']
            t = TIMERS[r]

            # holding the leftmost pad abandons the row; show it filling
            t0 = self.held_since(pad(r, 0), M_POMO)
            if t0 is not None:
                frac = (now - t0) / RESET_HOLD
                if frac >= 1.0:
                    self.rows[r] = {'state': IDLE, 'started': 0.0}
                    self.held.pop(pad(r, 0), None)
                    self.save()
                    for c in range(CELLS):
                        self.set(pad(r, c), t.idle if c == 0 else OFF)
                    continue
                if frac > 0.08:                # ignore an ordinary tap
                    n = int(frac * CELLS) + 1
                    for c in range(CELLS):
                        self.set(pad(r, c), RED if c < n else OFF)
                    continue
            # tick() always runs first and owns the elapsed handover (and its
            # chime) -- doing it in both meant two places could fire the same
            # transition.
            if s == RUNNING:
                done = min(int((now - st['started']) // t.step), CELLS - 1)
                for c in range(CELLS):
                    p = pad(r, c)
                    if c < done:    self.set(p, t.fill)
                    elif c == done: self.flash(p, t.fill)
                    else:           self.set(p, OFF)
            elif s == ELAPSED:
                for c in range(CELLS):
                    self.set(pad(r, c), t.fill if c == CELLS - 1 else RED)
            elif s == CLAIMED:
                for c in range(CELLS):
                    self.set(pad(r, c), t.fill)
            else:                                  # IDLE, and anything unknown
                for c in range(CELLS):
                    self.set(pad(r, c), t.idle if c == 0 else OFF)

    def render_toggles(self):
        for r in TOGGLE_ROWS:
            for c in range(CELLS):
                v = self.toggles.get((r, c), 0)
                self.set(pad(r, c), (OFF, RED, GREEN)[v])

    # --- input -------------------------------------------------------------
    def tick(self):
        """Advance every mode's state, whether or not it is on screen. A
        pomodoro must finish (and chime) while you are looking at another mode."""
        now = time.time()
        self.spectrum.reap(now)

        # habit gestures: holding a cell walks it through its states, one step
        # per second. Only pads pressed on THIS tab count -- a pad still down
        # from another tab is not a gesture here.
        if self.mode in HAB_MODES:
            for note in list(self.held):
                t0 = self.held_since(note)
                got = self.rc(note)
                if t0 is None or not got or widget_at(self.mode, got[0]) is not Widget.HABIT:
                    continue
                due = int((now - t0) / HOLD_CYCLE)      # one cycle per second held
                done = self._hold_count.get(note, 0)
                if due > done:
                    self._hold_count[note] = due
                    for _ in range(due - done):
                        self.cycle_state(*got)
        for r in TIMER_ROWS:
            st = self.rows[r]
            t = TIMERS[r]
            if st['state'] != RUNNING:
                continue
            elapsed = now - st['started']
            warn = t.warn
            if warn and not st.get('warned') and elapsed >= warn[0]:
                # flag persisted with the row, so a restart mid-break does not
                # replay a warning you have already heard
                st['warned'] = True
                self.save()
                ding(warn[1])
            if elapsed >= t.length:
                st['state'] = ELAPSED
                self.save()
                ding(t.chime)

    # ---- mode 2: system monitor -----------------------------------------
    def cpu_usage(self):
        """Per-CPU busy fraction since the previous call."""
        cur = {}
        for line in open('/proc/stat'):
            if not line.startswith('cpu') or line.startswith('cpu '):
                continue
            f = line.split()
            n = int(f[0][3:])
            vals = [int(x) for x in f[1:]]
            idle = vals[3] + vals[4]           # idle + iowait
            cur[n] = (sum(vals), idle)
        out = {}
        if self._cpu_prev:
            for n, (tot, idle) in cur.items():
                pt, pi = self._cpu_prev.get(n, (tot, idle))
                dt, di = tot - pt, idle - pi
                out[n] = max(0.0, min(1.0, (dt - di) / dt)) if dt > 0 else 0.0
        self._cpu_prev = cur
        return out

    def mem_usage(self):
        m = {}
        for l in open('/proc/meminfo'):
            k, v = l.split(':', 1)
            m[k] = int(v.split()[0])
        tot = m['MemTotal']
        buf = m['Buffers']
        cache = m['Cached'] + m['SReclaimable'] - m['Shmem']
        used = tot - m['MemFree'] - buf - cache
        return used / tot, buf / tot, cache / tot

    # ---- mode: network ---------------------------------------------------
    def net_iface(self):
        global NET_IFACE
        if NET_IFACE:
            return NET_IFACE
        try:
            for line in open('/proc/net/route'):
                f = line.split()
                if len(f) > 2 and f[1] == '00000000':      # default route
                    NET_IFACE = f[0]
                    return NET_IFACE
        except Exception:
            pass
        NET_IFACE = 'lo'
        return NET_IFACE

    def net_rates(self):
        """bytes/sec in and out since the previous call."""
        ifc = self.net_iface()
        rx = tx = 0
        try:
            for line in open('/proc/net/dev'):
                if line.split(':')[0].strip() == ifc:
                    f = line.split(':')[1].split()
                    rx, tx = int(f[0]), int(f[8])
                    break
        except Exception:
            pass
        now = time.time()
        prev = getattr(self, '_net_prev', None)
        self._net_prev = (now, rx, tx)
        if not prev:
            return 0.0, 0.0
        dt = max(1e-6, now - prev[0])
        return max(0, rx - prev[1]) / dt, max(0, tx - prev[2]) / dt

    def render_net(self):
        """Two half-width bars filling from the bottom. Each row is 4 pads, so a
        part-filled row gives 4x the resolution of whole rows alone. Log scale --
        network rates span decades and a linear bar would sit at zero all day."""
        import math
        LO, HI = 1e3, 1.25e8                       # 1 KB/s .. ~1 Gbit/s
        rows = list(reversed(CPU_ROWS + [MEM_ROW]))   # bottom-up, 7 rows
        half = CELLS // 2
        steps = len(rows) * half
        rx, tx = self.net_rates()
        for side, (rate, colour) in enumerate(((rx, CYAN), (tx, ORANGE))):
            frac = 0.0 if rate <= LO else min(1.0, math.log10(rate / LO) / math.log10(HI / LO))
            lit = int(round(frac * steps))
            for i, r in enumerate(rows):
                for j in range(half):
                    n = i * half + j
                    self.set(pad(r, side * half + j), colour if n < lit else OFF)

    def render_sys(self):
        # memory across the bottom row, htop's colours: used green,
        # buffers blue, cache yellow
        used, buf, cache = self.mem_usage()
        # each pad takes the colour of whichever segment its midpoint lands in
        b1, b2, b3 = used * CELLS, (used + buf) * CELLS, (used + buf + cache) * CELLS
        for c in range(CELLS):
            mid = c + 0.5
            colour = (GREEN if mid < b1 else
                      BLUE  if mid < b2 else
                      YELLOW if mid < b3 else OFF)
            self.set(pad(MEM_ROW, c), colour)

        # CPU: 8 columns, each the MAX of its four threads, 6 pads tall
        u = self.cpu_usage()
        per = max(1, (len(u) or 32) // CELLS)
        for c in range(CELLS):
            group = [u.get(c * per + i, 0.0) for i in range(per)]
            v = max(group) if group else 0.0
            lit = int(round(v * len(CPU_ROWS)))
            for i, r in enumerate(reversed(CPU_ROWS)):   # fill upward
                if i < lit:
                    colour = GREEN if i < 4 else (YELLOW if i < 5 else RED)
                else:
                    colour = OFF
                self.set(pad(r, c), colour)

    def rc(self, note):
        """note -> (logical row, col), or None if it isn't a grid pad."""
        return RC.get(note)

    def release(self, note):
        got = self.held.pop(note, None)
        self._hold_count.pop(note, None)         # always drop it; it leaked when
                                                 # a pad was let go on another tab

        if note == pad(FUNC_ROW, RESET_COL) and got is not None:
            # One pad, two jobs, told apart purely by how long it was down:
            # a quick tap dismisses the habit window, a 2s hold resets the tab.
            if time.time() - got[0] >= RESET_HOLD:
                self.reset()
            elif self._editing:
                self._popup_cmd('hide')
                self._editing = None
            return

        # Habit selection happens on press, so there is nothing left to do here.
        # Releasing only ends the hold, which tick() has already been acting on.

    def press(self, note):
        # Tagged with the tab that was showing when the pad went down, so a pad
        # still held across a tab change cannot be read as a gesture on the new
        # tab. See held_since().
        self.held[note] = (time.time(), self.mode)
        self._hold_count.pop(note, None)
        got = self.rc(note)
        if not got:
            return                             # not one of the 64 grid pads
        lr, lc = got
        if lr == FUNC_ROW:
            if lc in TABS:                     # reset pad is hold-only, see render()
                self.mode = lc
                if self._editing:
                    if lc in HAB_MODES:
                        # window follows the tab: swap the whole table
                        self._popup_mode = lc
                        self._popup_send_table('load')
                        er, ec = self._editing
                        self._popup_cmd(f'focus\t{er}\t{ec}')
                    else:
                        self._popup_cmd('hide')   # left the habit tabs
                        self._editing = None
            return
        widget = widget_at(self.mode, lr)
        if widget is Widget.HABIT:
            # Select on the way DOWN, not on release: holding a pad that isn't
            # the selected one cycles it immediately, so waiting for release
            # meant watching a cell change while a different one stayed lit.
            # A hold is then just a select that also cycles.
            self.select_habit(lr, lc)
        elif widget is Widget.TIMER:
            self.pomo_press(lr, lc)
        elif widget is Widget.CONTROL:
            self.control_press(lc)
        elif widget is Widget.TOGGLE:
            self.toggles[(lr, lc)] = (self.toggles.get((lr, lc), 0) + 1) % 3
            self.save()
        # The read-only widgets -- the bar and the meters -- and any row a tab
        # leaves dark have no gesture at all, by construction rather than by a
        # guard that someone has to remember to write.

    def reset(self):
        """Reset only what the CURRENT mode owns.

        This used to reset the pomodoros for any non-habit mode, so holding the
        red pad while looking at the CPU or network meters silently wiped every
        running timer with nothing on screen to suggest it had.
        """
        if self.mode in HAB_MODES:
            for h in self.habits.values():
                h['state'] = 0
            self._popup_send_table()           # the window shows state too
        elif self.mode == M_POMO:
            for r in TIMER_ROWS:
                self.rows[r] = {'state': IDLE, 'started': 0.0}
            self.toggles = {}
        else:
            return                             # the meters have nothing to reset
        self.save()

    # ---- mode 3: habit tracker -------------------------------------------
    def habit_table(self, mode):
        """The table belonging to one habit tab, addressed explicitly."""
        if mode not in HAB_MODES:
            return {}
        return self.habit_sets.setdefault(str(mode), {})

    @property
    def habits(self):
        """The table for the SELECTED tab, for drawing. Only habit modes have
        one. Using setdefault() unconditionally created a stray entry for
        whatever mode happened to be selected."""
        return self.habit_table(self.mode)

    HAB_STATE_COLOUR = {2: GREEN}              # 0 and 1 are handled in render_habits

    def habit(self, r, c, mode=None):
        tbl = self.habits if mode is None else self.habit_table(mode)
        return tbl.get(f'{r},{c}', {'name': '', 'colour': WHITE, 'state': 0})

    def set_habit(self, r, c, h, mode=None):
        mode = self.mode if mode is None else mode
        tbl = self.habit_table(mode)
        k = f'{r},{c}'
        # The popup reader thread also lands here, and _flush() serialises
        # habit_sets: mutate under the same lock or json.dumps() can trip over
        # a resized dict and lose the whole write.
        with self._lock:
            if h.get('name'):
                tbl[k] = h
            else:
                tbl.pop(k, None)               # empty name = habit deleted
        self.save()
        if mode == self._popup_mode:
            self._popup_send_table()

    def render_habits(self):
        for r in HAB_ROWS:
            for c in range(CELLS):
                h = self.habit(r, c)
                own = h.get('colour', WHITE)
                if not h['name']:
                    col = OFF                  # a habit with no name does not exist
                elif h.get('state', 0) == 1:
                    # in progress: alternate red and the habit's own colour, so a
                    # started habit is the only thing on the board that moves and
                    # still shows which habit it is
                    col = RED if int(time.time() / HAB_FLASH) % 2 else own
                else:
                    col = self.HAB_STATE_COLOUR.get(h.get('state', 0), own)
                self.set(pad(r, c), col)

    def select_habit(self, r, c):
        """Make (r,c) the selected cell, opening the window if it is closed."""
        if self._editing == (r, c):
            return                              # already selected; Esc closes
        if self._editing:
            self._editing = (r, c)
            self._popup_cmd(f'focus\t{r}\t{c}')
        else:
            self.edit_habit(r, c)

    def edit_habit(self, r, c):
        """Open the window with this cell selected. Esc is the only way out."""
        self._popup_mode = self.mode
        self._popup_send_table()
        self._popup_cmd(f'edit\t{r}\t{c}')
        self._editing = (r, c)

    def cycle_state(self, r, c, mode=None):
        h = dict(self.habit(r, c, mode))
        if not h['name']:
            return
        h['state'] = (h.get('state', 0) + 1) % 3   # unstarted -> started -> complete
        self.set_habit(r, c, h, mode)              # already repaints the window

    def pomo_press(self, r, c):
        st = self.rows[r]; s = st['state']
        if s == IDLE and c == 0:
            # warned=False matters: update() keeps existing keys, so without it
            # a second break would inherit the first one's spent warning flag
            st.update(state=RUNNING, started=time.time(), warned=False)
            ding('start.wav')
        elif s == ELAPSED:
            if c == CELLS - 1: st['state'] = CLAIMED       # claimed it
            else:              st.update(state=IDLE, started=0)   # failed
        elif s == CLAIMED and c == 0:
            st.update(state=IDLE, started=0)
        self.save()


def main():
    # systemd stops the unit with SIGTERM, whose default action kills the
    # process outright: the finally: below never ran and up to a second of
    # state -- a just-started pomodoro -- went with it on every restart.
    def _term(signum, frame):
        raise KeyboardInterrupt
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _term)

    names = [n for n in mido.get_output_names() if 'MK3' in n or 'Launchpad' in n]
    ins   = [n for n in mido.get_input_names()  if 'MK3' in n or 'Launchpad' in n]
    # the MIDI (not DAW) port is the one that honours programmer mode
    def pick(lst):
        for n in lst:
            if 'MI' in n: return n
        return lst[0] if lst else None
    o, i = pick(names), pick(ins)
    if not o or not i:
        sys.exit(f"Launchpad not found.\n  outputs: {mido.get_output_names()}\n  inputs: {mido.get_input_names()}")
    print(f"out: {o}\nin : {i}")
    with mido.open_output(o) as out, mido.open_input(i) as inp:
        out.send(mido.Message('sysex', data=SYSEX_PROGRAMMER))
        time.sleep(0.1)
        b = Board(out); b.clear()
        try:
            while True:
                for m in inp.iter_pending():
                    if m.type == 'note_on' and m.velocity > 0:
                        b.press(m.note)
                    elif m.type == 'note_off' or (m.type == 'note_on' and m.velocity == 0):
                        b.release(m.note)
                try:
                    b.tick()
                    b.render()
                except Exception as e:
                    # keep the board alive; a bad frame is not worth a restart
                    print(f"tick/render error: {e!r}", flush=True)
                    time.sleep(0.5)
                time.sleep(SPEC_TICK if b.mode == M_SPEC else TICK)
        except KeyboardInterrupt:
            pass
        finally:
            # Persist FIRST. Anything below is cosmetic teardown that can fail
            # (a dead popup, a yanked USB cable), and when it did, _flush never
            # ran and a running pomodoro was silently lost.
            try:
                b._flush()
            except Exception as e:
                print(f"could not save state: {e!r}", flush=True)
            try:
                b._popup_cmd('hide')
                b.clear(); time.sleep(0.05)
                out.send(mido.Message('sysex', data=SYSEX_LIVE))
            except Exception:
                pass
            print("\nexited, launchpad restored")

if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
